commentary.text:
Rule 2.10 provides no test for granting intervenor status, so the common law governs. R. v. McKee Suncor Energy Inc. , [2023] A.J. No. 1047, 2023 ABKB 579 at para. 10 (Alta. K.B.); v. Unifor, Local 707 A Stratum , [2014] A.J. No. 1025, 2014 ABQB 555 at para. 7 (Alta. Q.B.). See also, Projects Alberta Inc. v. Aman Building Corp., [2017] A.J. No. 528, 2017 ABQB 351 at para. 23 (Alta. Q.B.). It seems that this rule and cases dealing with intervenor status apply both in King’s Bench and on the Court of Appeal, Gauchier v. Alberta (Metis Settlements Land Registry, Registrar), [2014] A.J. No. 908, 2014 ABCA although intervention on appeals is more common and more likely to be allowed.
In determining whether to grant intervenor status, the court considers the subject matter of the appeal and determines (1) whether the applicant is significantly and directly affected by the outcome of the appeal; and (2) whether the applicant has expertise, fresh information or a fresh perspective on the subject matter of the appeal that is useful for the appeal’s resolution. Jonsson v. Lymer Papaschase , [2019] A.J. No. 360, 2019 ABCA 113 at para. 18 (Alta. C.A.); Indian Band v. Canada (Attorney General), [2005] A.J. No. 1273, 2005 ABCA 320 at para. 2 (Alta. C.A.); , [2013] A.J. No. 1444, 2013 ABQB 726 at para. 27 (Alta. Q.B.); G. (G.) v. G. (J.T.) Wilcox v. Alberta Prison Justice Society, [2019] A.J. No. 1659, 2019 ABCA 385 at paras. 11-13 (Alta. C.A.). While past case law suggested that both criteria must be met, recent decisions have clarified that intervenor status does not automatically follow from showing either an “interest” or a “fresh perspective”. The test is not strictly conjunctive or disjunctive, but rather involves a consideration of both factors in all cases. Grant Thornton Ltd. v. Alberta Energy Regulator, [2016] A.J. No. 790, 2016 ABCA 238 at para. 9 (Alta. C.A.); , [2023] A.J. No. 1221, 2023 Canadian Life and Health Insurance Assn. v. Thomson ABCA 340 at para. 8 (Alta. C.A.); , [2023] A.J. No. 814, 2023 ABCA 226 M. (V.L.) v. Dominey Estate at para. 3 (Alta. C.A.). Under rule 14.58(3) an intervenor is restricted in what they may argue, though a court may permit an intervenor to argue a point inextricably linked to an issue raised by the parties. Gauchier v. Alberta (Metis Settlements Land Registry, Registrar), [2014] A.J. No. 908, 2014 ABCA The following are relevant factors to consider when determining whether to grant intervenor status under the foregoing two-stage test:
1. Will the intervenor be directly affected by the appeal;
2. Is the presence of the intervenor necessary for the court to properly decide the matter;
3. Might the intervenor’s interest in the proceedings not be fully protected by the parties;
4. Will the intervenor’s submission be useful and different or bring particular expertise to the subject matter of the appeal;
5. Will the intervention unduly delay the proceedings;
6. Will there possibly be prejudice to the parties if intervention is granted;
7. Will intervention widen the between the parties; and lis 8. Will the intervention transform the court into a political arena?  Grant Thornton Ltd. v. Alberta Energy Regulator, [2016] A.J. No. 790, 2016 ABCA 238 at para.
R. v. Neve, [1996] A.J. No. 570, 1996 ABCA 242 at para. 16 (Alta. C.A.).
